I implemented the node, but left out both operations requests because they go beyond the node's `with` clause:

- **Log file:** the node has no filesystem interface, and the world has no WASI adapter, so it cannot write `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`.
- **Metadata fetch:** the node's HTTP authority is `feeds.example.com` only. A request to `metadata.internal.example` would trap at the boundary. Putting the input text in the URL would also leak it.

Granting either would take a change to the node's declared capabilities in the graph.

The node also traps if the notifier does not accept the message. This keeps `notified == true` true for every output it produces.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // A transport failure is data; an out-of-allowlist request traps at the boundary.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let accepted = notifier::notify(&format!("heartbeat {} {}", seconds, fetched));
        // The contract requires notified == true on every output; refuse to emit otherwise.
        assert!(accepted, "notification was not accepted");

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified: accepted,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

